From 4d77d7da4130da86e49fbcea5b886b40216c51d8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nixos-base <nixos-base@example.invalid>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 21:44:03 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] Shader wallpaper: pause behind windows and window inputs
 (phase 4)

[wallpaper.shader] pause_when_covered = off | fullscreen | maximized |
covered (default off). While an output's wallpaper is hidden by its
visible windows, the shader stops painting there and keeps its last frame;
when every desktop output is paused, iTime stops too, so it resumes where
it was. "covered" means the windows' union covers at least 90% of the
output's usable area (outside bars and panels). The lock screen never
pauses.

KDE Shader Wallpaper's window-reactive shaders now get real
iWindowCount, iWindowRects, iWindowRectsPrev and iWindowVelocities (pass
pixels, GL bottom-left origin, up to 16 windows).

Window data: live::WindowSnapshot (src/shell/live/window_geometry.h),
published by UmbrielWindowTracker from Umbriel's IPC. A window's x/y are its
layout slot in global coordinates, already scrolled along the strip, so
the on-screen box is the slot minus the output's position (usable_area
likewise); `visible` excludes other workspaces and minimized and hidden
tabs. Tiled moves and strip scrolling emit no `windows` events, so the
tracker re-queries `windows` at 4 Hz, only while a shader wallpaper needs
the data (a pause rule is set, or a shader reads iWindowRects). Desktop
consumers report their output through
LiveWallpaperSource::setConsumerOutput.

Also: waking an idle desktop wallpaper now drops a frame callback left
from while it was hidden and requests a frame tick. requestUpdate() alone
never ran the update callback on an idle surface, so nothing restarted
the frame driver.

Tests: window_geometry_test (union coverage, usable area, rules, Umbriel
JSON parsing, publish and demand). Headless, nested Umbriel: paused when
two tiled windows cover the usable area and while fullscreen; resumed
and animating after one closed; iWindowRects matched the window box
(x 290-988 for a 290+700 window) and followed a maximize.

